<script lang="ts">
	// ── SVX prototype ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	// This whole page is ONE .svx file: markdown + <script> + live Svelte components,
	// compiled by mdsvex. The point is to prove that fractalsvelte's docs can be
	// authored as markdown and still carry fully interactive, stateful previews.
	import { Button } from "$lib/components/button/index.js";
	import { Badge } from "$lib/components/badge/index.js";
	import {
		Card,
		CardAction,
		CardContent,
		CardDescription,
		CardFooter,
		CardHeader,
		CardTitle,
	} from "$lib/components/card/index.js";
	import { Tabs, TabsContent, TabsList, TabsTrigger } from "$lib/components/tabs/index.js";
	import {
		Accordion,
		AccordionContent,
		AccordionItem,
		AccordionTrigger,
	} from "$lib/components/accordion/index.js";
	import { Switch } from "$lib/components/switch/index.js";
	import { Checkbox } from "$lib/components/checkbox/index.js";
	import { Slider } from "$lib/components/slider/index.js";
	import { Progress } from "$lib/components/progress/index.js";
	import {
		Tooltip,
		TooltipContent,
		TooltipProvider,
		TooltipTrigger,
	} from "$lib/components/tooltip/index.js";
	import {
		Dialog,
		DialogContent,
		DialogDescription,
		DialogFooter,
		DialogHeader,
		DialogTitle,
		DialogTrigger,
	} from "$lib/components/dialog/index.js";
	import { Carousel, CarouselContent, CarouselItem, CarouselNext, CarouselPrevious } from "$lib/components/carousel/index.js";
	import { Toaster, toast } from "$lib/components/sonner/index.js";
	import Preview from "$lib/docs/Preview.svelte";
	import Examples from "$lib/docs/Examples.svelte";
	import CodeBlock from "$lib/docs/CodeBlock.svelte";
	import { byCategory, progress } from "$lib/docs/registry.js";

	import { SvelteFlow, Background } from "@xyflow/svelte";
	import "@xyflow/svelte/dist/style.css";
	import { browser } from "$app/environment";

	// ── Shared state lives right here in the markdown ─────────────────────────────
	let notifications = $state(true);
	let termsAccepted = $state(false);
	let volume = $state([45]);
	let tab = $state("overview");
	let dialogOpen = $state(false);
	let demoProgress = $state(64);

	// String literals for inline prose live here: mdsvex's markdown pass smart-quotes
	// double quotes inside text nodes, which would break inline {expr} ternaries.
	const bellState = $derived(notifications ? "ON" : "OFF");
	function statusLabel(status: string, wave: number) {
		return status === "ready" ? "Ready" : `Wave ${wave}`;
	}

	const { ready, total } = progress();
	const groups = byCategory();

	const flowNodes = [
		{ id: "layout", position: { x: 0, y: 30 }, data: { label: "+layout.svelte" } },
		{ id: "page", position: { x: 220, y: 30 }, data: { label: "+page.svx" } },
		{ id: "button", position: { x: 440, y: 30 }, data: { label: "Button" } },
	];
	const flowEdges = [
		{ id: "e1", source: "layout", target: "page", animated: true },
		{ id: "e2", source: "page", target: "button", animated: true },
	];

	const slides = ["One", "Two", "Three", "Four", "Five"];

	const codeInterop = `<script lang="ts">
  import { Button } from "$lib/components/button/index.js";
  let count = $state(0);
<\/script>

<Button onclick={() => count++}>
  Clicked {count} {count === 1 ? "time" : "times"}
</Button>`;
</script>

<svelte:head>
	<title>SVX Prototype — fractalsvelte</title>
	<meta
		name="description"
		content="A single .svx file with fully interactive live previews of fractalsvelte components, rendered by mdsvex in SvelteKit."
	/>
</svelte:head>

<article class="doc-article svx-prototype">

<h1 class="doc-title">SVX on mdsvex — live previews in markdown</h1>

<p class="doc-lede">
	This page is <strong>one <code>.svx</code> file</strong>. Everything below — the prose, the
	state, the interactive components, even the graph — is authored in a single markdown
	document and compiled by <code>mdsvex</code>. Markdown is the source of truth; the
	components are live, not screenshots.
</p>

## 1. A preview that reacts to markdown state

This is plain markdown, but it reads live Svelte state declared in the script block above:

> Notification bell is currently **{bellState}** and the volume slider is at
> **{volume[0]}%**.

The prose below is the same document as the widgets — flip the switch or drag the slider and
the sentence changes with it:

<div class="doc-stage row wrap ycenter gap24">

<Switch bind:checked={notifications} />

<div class="box gap8">
	<label class="text-sm" for="volume-slider">Volume: {volume[0]}%</label>
	<Slider id="volume-slider" bind:value={volume} max={100} step={5} class="w240" />
</div>

<Checkbox bind:checked={termsAccepted}>
	I accept the terms (this binds too)
</Checkbox>

</div>

<Preview code={codeInterop} description="Stateful interop — a counter bound inside markdown">
	<Button onclick={() => (demoProgress = Math.min(100, demoProgress + 8))}
		>Bump progress: {demoProgress}%</Button
	>
	<Progress value={demoProgress} class="w240" />
</Preview>

## 2. An interactive component matrix

Tabs, accordion, tooltips, a dialog and toasts — all rendered live from the library:

<Tabs bind:value={tab}>
	<TabsList>
		<TabsTrigger value="overview">Overview</TabsTrigger>
		<TabsTrigger value="tokens">Tokens</TabsTrigger>
		<TabsTrigger value="code">Code</TabsTrigger>
	</TabsList>
	<TabsContent value="overview">
		<div class="doc-stage row wrap ycenter gap16">
			<Button>default</Button>
			<Button variant="outline">outline</Button>
			<Button variant="secondary">secondary</Button>
			<Button variant="ghost">ghost</Button>
			<Button variant="destructive">destructive</Button>
			<Button variant="link">link</Button>
			<Badge>badge</Badge>
			<Badge variant="secondary">secondary</Badge>
			<Badge variant="outline">outline</Badge>
		</div>
	</TabsContent>
	<TabsContent value="tokens">
		<div class="box gap8">
			<p class="text-sm">
				Token-driven theming: every color comes from CSS custom properties, so a
				preview can be re-themed by an ancestor without touching component code.
			</p>
			<ul class="box gap8">
				<li><code>--primary</code> — default variant fill</li>
				<li><code>--background</code> — surfaces</li>
				<li><code>--border</code> — hairline strokes</li>
				<li><code>--ring</code> — focus</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</TabsContent>
	<TabsContent value="code">
		<p class="text-sm">The tab bar itself is a fractalsvelte <code>Tabs</code> component.</p>
	</TabsContent>
</Tabs>

<Accordion type="multiple" value={["item-1"]}>
	<AccordionItem value="item-1">
		<AccordionTrigger>Why does this work in markdown?</AccordionTrigger>
		<AccordionContent>
			<p>
				mdsvex compiles <code>.svx</code>/<code>.md</code> files into Svelte components.
				Script blocks become instance code, headings become HTML, and any component you
				import can be dropped inline — exactly like MDX, but for Svelte.
			</p>
		</AccordionContent>
	</AccordionItem>
	<AccordionItem value="item-2">
		<AccordionTrigger>Is the demo state isolated per page?</AccordionTrigger>
		<AccordionContent>
			<p>
				Yes — each doc page compiles to its own component instance, so two pages can
				use the same snippets without sharing state.
			</p>
		</AccordionContent>
	</AccordionItem>
</Accordion>

<div class="doc-stage row wrap ycenter gap16">

{#snippet tooltipTrigger({ props })}
	<Button variant="outline" {...props}>Hover me</Button>
{/snippet}

{#snippet dialogTrigger({ props })}
	<Button variant="outline" {...props}>Open dialog</Button>
{/snippet}

<TooltipProvider>
	<Tooltip>
		<TooltipTrigger child={tooltipTrigger} />
		<TooltipContent>Tooltip content, live.</TooltipContent>
	</Tooltip>
</TooltipProvider>

<Dialog bind:open={dialogOpen}>
	<DialogTrigger child={dialogTrigger} />
	<DialogContent>
		<DialogHeader>
			<DialogTitle>SVX prototype</DialogTitle>
			<DialogDescription>
				A modal rendered from a heading inside markdown — no separate page needed.
			</DialogDescription>
		</DialogHeader>
		<p class="text-sm">
			Overlays, portals, focus traps and scroll locking all work because this is a real
			Svelte component tree.
		</p>
		<DialogFooter>
			<Button variant="outline" onclick={() => (dialogOpen = false)}>Cancel</Button>
			<Button onclick={() => (dialogOpen = false)}>Confirm</Button>
		</DialogFooter>
	</DialogContent>
</Dialog>

<Button variant="outline" onclick={() => toast("Toast from markdown", { description: "sonner renders in the root layout." })}>
	Fire a toast
</Button>

</div>

## 3. Data-driven previews — the registry in markdown

Because the script block has full access to the app's modules, a doc page can render
<em>live library data</em>, not hardcoded lists. Below, the component registry drives the UI:

<div class="doc-note box">
	<strong>{ready}</strong> of <strong>{total}</strong> registry components are <code>ready</code>.
</div>

{#each groups.filter((g) => g.items.length) as group (group.category)}
	<h3>{group.category}</h3>
	<div class="doc-index-grid">
		{#each group.items.slice(0, 6) as item (item.slug)}
			<a class="doc-index-card box" href="/components/{item.slug}" data-status={item.status}>
				<strong>{item.name}</strong>
				<span>{statusLabel(item.status, item.wave)}</span>
			</a>
		{/each}
	</div>
{/each}

## 4. Bonus — a graph inside markdown

The earlier question: can a SvelteKit site show repo/layout/info-flow with xyflow? Here's the
answer rendered <em>inside a markdown document</em> — a draggable <code>@xyflow/svelte</code>
graph with zero page-level Svelte code:

<div class="svx-flow" style="height: 240px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px;">
	{#if browser}
		<SvelteFlow nodes={flowNodes} edges={flowEdges} fitView nodesDraggable={true}>
			<Background />
		</SvelteFlow>
	{:else}
		<p class="text-sm muted">Graph renders client-side only.</p>
	{/if}
</div>

## Verdict

Good live previews for `@packages/fractalsvelte` are not just possible — they're already the
pattern. Every one of the 70+ component pages under `src/content/components/` is a markdown
file carrying <strong>real, interactive previews</strong> (this page is the `.svx` twin that
adds <code>#snippet</code>-based <code>Examples</code>, state binding, and third-party
components to the mix). Markdown authors get prose; users get working demos.

</article>
